WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.109
Your server lives inside a VPC, your own private network in AWS.

2
00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:15.087
The default VPC is the block 172.31.0.0/16.

3
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.352
Each availability zone gets one /20 subnet.

4
00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:24.252
That is 4,096 addresses, minus the 5 that AWS keeps.

5
00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:27.948
Your server lands in one of them.

6
00:00:27.948 --> 00:00:32.800
It only knows its private address, 172.31.0.5.

7
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:40.366
On it, gunicorn listens on 0.0.0.0, port 8000, so outside traffic can reach it.

8
00:00:40.366 --> 00:00:43.244
Something else decides who may.

9
00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:46.751
An internet gateway is attached to the VPC.

10
00:00:46.751 --> 00:00:48.813
It is the VPC's front door.

11
00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:51.913
The subnet's route table has two rules.

12
00:00:51.913 --> 00:00:54.572
Addresses inside the VPC stay local.

13
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.123
And 0.0.0.0/0, which means every address, goes to the internet gateway.

14
00:01:01.123 --> 00:01:05.309
That one route is what makes the subnet public.

15
00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:09.257
The server also gets a public address, but it never sees it.

16
00:01:09.257 --> 00:01:13.291
The internet gateway holds the pair, and swaps one for the other.

17
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.752
Last, the firewall.

18
00:01:14.752 --> 00:01:16.992
A security group wraps the server.

19
00:01:16.992 --> 00:01:21.669
The lab's group, hello-devops-web, starts with no inbound rules at all.

20
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.348
Now send one request from your Mac, to the public address, on port 8000.

21
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:32.309
At the internet gateway, the public destination is swapped for the private one.

22
00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:35.330
Inside, it reaches the security group.

23
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.568
No rule allows it, so the packet is dropped.

24
00:01:38.568 --> 00:01:39.231
Silently.

25
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:42.522
Your curl hears nothing back, and times out.

26
00:01:42.522 --> 00:01:44.980
Day 10 said a timeout means a drop.

27
00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.173
Here is the drop.

28
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.654
So add one inbound rule.

29
00:01:48.654 --> 00:01:53.539
TCP port 8000, from your own address only, as a /32.

30
00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:54.689
Send it again.

31
00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:56.666
The gateway swaps the address.

32
00:01:56.666 --> 00:02:00.421
The security group finds a matching rule, and lets it in.

33
00:02:00.633 --> 00:02:01.754
gunicorn answers.

34
00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:04.061
The reply needs no rule of its own.

35
00:02:04.061 --> 00:02:06.105
The security group is stateful.

36
00:02:06.105 --> 00:02:09.599
It remembers the connection, and lets the answer out.

37
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:20.367
Your Mac's address is outside the VPC, so the only route that matches is 0.0.0.0/0, to the internet gateway.

38
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.991
The gateway swaps the private source back to the public one.

39
00:02:23.991 --> 00:02:27.099
So your Mac gets its answer from the address it called.

40
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.182
Port 8000 is open to you alone, and port 22 stays closed.

41
00:02:32.182 --> 00:02:35.047
So how do you get a shell on the server?

42
00:02:35.267 --> 00:02:36.954
With Session Manager.

43
00:02:36.954 --> 00:02:45.392
A small program on the server, the SSM Agent, keeps an outbound HTTPS connection open to Systems Manager.

44
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.565
Outbound is allowed by default.

45
00:02:47.565 --> 00:02:53.080
And because the security group is stateful, traffic on that connection flows both ways.

46
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.659
On your Mac, you run start-session.

47
00:02:55.659 --> 00:03:00.780
AWS checks your IAM permissions, and joins your terminal to that connection.

48
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.793
You arrive as ssm-user.

49
00:03:02.793 --> 00:03:05.600
No port to open, and no key to lose.

50
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:14.149
So one request: the route table points to the gateway, the gateway swaps the address, and the security group opens one port.

51
00:03:14.149 --> 00:03:17.717
And Session Manager dials out, so port 22 stays shut.
